(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-copd-smoking-cessation-quiz"]={"title":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, Smoking Cessation & Immunizations Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which regular therapy is acceptable on its own in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease but never on its own in asthma?","choices":["An inhaled corticosteroid","A leukotriene receptor antagonist","An anti-immunoglobulin E antibody","A long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In chronic obstructive pulmonary disease a long-acting muscarinic antagonist or a long-acting beta-2 agonist alone is regular treatment; in asthma a long-acting beta-2 agonist is never used alone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"In which form is nicotine polacrilex given?","choices":["Patch","Spray","Inhaler","Gum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nicotine polacrilex is the nicotine gum; with the patches, the gum is available over the counter. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"For which patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is long-term oxygen therapy listed?","choices":["Those with persistent hypoxemia","Those in the high-risk groups","Those with asthma overlap","Those who still smoke"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-term oxygen therapy is listed, for patients in any group, if there is chronic (persistent) hypoxemia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"In chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, when do symptoms generally develop?","choices":["Before lung function begins to decline","After a significant decline in lung function","As soon as lung function first falls","When lung function returns to normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms generally develop only after a significant decline in forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) has occurred, and they progress as lung function deteriorates further. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"In a patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, what can stopping smoking do to the decline in ventilatory function?","choices":["Return it to the normal age-related rate","Reverse the existing obstruction fully","Stop age-related decline entirely","Speed it up for the first year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation can return the decline in ventilatory function to the normal age-related rate and reduce the risk of lung cancer. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which black box warning is listed for varenicline?","choices":["Lowered seizure threshold","Cardiac tachyarrhythmias","Neuropsychiatric events","Severe liver toxicity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline's black box warning is for neuropsychiatric events such as depression and suicidal ideation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"How is bupropion thought to help a smoker quit?","choices":["It partially stimulates nicotine receptors","It replaces nicotine to ease withdrawal","It inhibits norepinephrine and dopamine uptake","It blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nicotine indirectly activates epinephrine and dopamine in the central nervous system, and bupropion is postulated to mimic these effects because it inhibits the uptake of norepinephrine and dopamine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What determines the dose and duration of nicotine replacement therapy?","choices":["Body weight and kidney function","The age at which smoking began","The number of past quit attempts","Dependence level and stage of treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The dose and duration of nicotine replacement depend on the patient's level of dependence and point in therapy; the duration is between 6 and 12 weeks. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which smoking cessation agent is a partial nicotine receptor agonist?","choices":["Bupropion","Nicotine polacrilex","Nicotine transdermal system","Varenicline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline (Chantix) is a partial nicotine receptor agonist, acting to a lesser degree than nicotine. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Nicotine replacement calls for caution in which patients?","choices":["Those with cardiovascular problems","Those with a seizure disorder","Those taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor","Those with a history of depression"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nicotine replacement calls for caution in patients with cardiovascular problems because of the chance of tachyarrhythmias and hypertension. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which nicotine replacement forms are available over the counter?","choices":["Spray and inhaler","Patches and gum","Gum and spray","Patches and inhaler"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nicotine patches and gum are available over the counter; the spray and the inhaler are the other listed nicotine forms. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A high-risk patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has severe breathlessness and no features of asthma. What is the suggested regular treatment?","choices":["A long-acting muscarinic antagonist alone","A short-acting bronchodilator as needed","A long-acting beta-2 agonist plus muscarinic antagonist","An inhaled glucocorticoid with a long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Group D (more symptomatic, high risk) gets a long-acting muscarinic antagonist or, if breathlessness is severe, the combination of a long-acting beta-2 agonist and a long-acting muscarinic antagonist. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease must stop to catch breath when walking on level ground and had one exacerbation last year without hospitalization. What is the suggested regular treatment?","choices":["A short-acting bronchodilator as needed alone","A long-acting muscarinic antagonist or beta-2 agonist","An inhaled glucocorticoid with a long-acting beta-2 agonist","A long-acting beta-2 agonist plus muscarinic antagonist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Group B (more symptomatic, low risk) gets regular treatment with one long-acting bronchodilator, either a muscarinic antagonist or a beta-2 agonist by patient preference, plus a short-acting bronchodilator as needed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has frequent hospitalized exacerbations, marked breathlessness and features that overlap with asthma. Which inhaler may be preferred?","choices":["A long-acting beta-2 agonist on its own","A long-acting muscarinic antagonist on its own","A glucocorticoid plus long-acting beta-2 agonist","A short-acting beta-2 agonist plus antimuscarinic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In group D a combination glucocorticoid-long-acting beta-2 agonist inhaler may be preferred if features of asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease overlap. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"}]};
